setup: |-
  const mockData = {
    outputMode: 'score',
    threshold: 70,
    blockAiCrawlers: false,
    trustedIpHeader: 'cf-connecting-ip',
    spamReferrers: '',
    geoHeader: '',
    inMarketRegions: '',
    highRiskRegions: '',
    suspectScreens: '',
    debug: false
  };
  const CHROME_UA = 'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/124.0.0.0 Safari/537.36';
  // hdr(map) -> getRequestHeader mock. Keys are lowercase header names.
  const hdr = (map) => (h) => map[h];
  // chrome(overrides) -> a clean, fully-populated Chrome request; pass a key
  // with value undefined to remove that header.
  const chrome = (overrides) => {
    const base = {
      'user-agent': CHROME_UA,
      'sec-ch-ua': '"Chromium";v="124", "Google Chrome";v="124", "Not.A/Brand";v="24"',
      'sec-ch-ua-platform': '"Windows"',
      'sec-ch-ua-mobile': '?0',
      'sec-fetch-mode': 'cors',
      'accept-language': 'en-US,en;q=0.9',
      'accept-encoding': 'gzip, deflate, br'
    };
    if (overrides) { for (const k in overrides) { base[k] = overrides[k]; } }
    return hdr(base);
  };
  // ev(map) -> getEventData mock.
  const ev = (map) => (k) => (map ? map[k] : undefined);


___NOTES___

Bot Score by adtech.solutions — server-side bot scoring for sGTM
(Stape / Taggrs / self-hosted / any sGTM host). v3.0.

WHAT IT GATES
  This variable scores the *measurement* request, not site access. Wire it as a
  blocking-trigger exception on the GA4 server tag (block when the score is
  >= your threshold) to keep bot hits out of GA4. It cannot stop a crawler
  from reading the site — that is robots.txt / WAF territory.

ROLLOUT — SHADOW MODE, THEN VALIDATE AGAINST REVENUE
  1. Add the variable in "score" mode and send it as a GA4 event parameter
     (e.g. bot_score). Use ONE variable per output type: never send a
     boolean-mode and a score-mode variable to the same parameter.
  2. After a few days, in BigQuery, group PURCHASE events by bot_score:

       SELECT (SELECT value.int_value FROM UNNEST(event_params)
                 WHERE key='bot_score') AS score,
              COUNT(*) purchases, SUM(ecommerce.purchase_revenue) revenue
       FROM `project.analytics_XXX.events_*`
       WHERE event_name='purchase' AND _TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN ... AND ...
       GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1;

     Any score band that contains real revenue must NEVER be blocked. On the
     retailer this was calibrated against, 49.5% of weekly revenue sat at
     exactly 25 under v2.1 (the old client-hints weight) and NOTHING legitimate
     scored above 25 — which is why the threshold stays at 70 and that weight
     was cut. Do this check again after any weight change and on every new
     site; in-app / WebView share differs a lot between markets.
  3. Only then add the blocking-trigger exception. Block with ">= threshold":
     a hit scoring exactly the threshold is a bot.

     v3.2 calibration (same retailer, GA4 export Aug-Nov 2026): of ~23k
     purchases recorded while nothing was blocked, none scored >= 50; purchases
     at 20/30 were Android tablets and desktop-site mode (the v3.1 fixes); a UA
     whose platform section ends in " )" covered 176k one-page "users" with 5
     purchases (all from 163 users at score 0). On that site 50 is a safe
     threshold; elsewhere keep 70 until the same check is done.
     Lesson from v3.2: "0 purchases in the band" is not enough to raise a
     weight. Chromium < 80 had 0 purchases in 10k "users", but at 25 it put
     real old phones (Chrome 61-79 cores, lt-lt, multi-page sessions) on
     exactly 50 within a day. Check which real combinations a new weight
     creates, not only the band it was measured on.

SIGNALS & WEIGHTS (see the W_* constants)
  Decisive alone (70): datacenter IP, spam referrer.
  Near-decisive (60): client_hints contradicting the UA; GA4 ip_override
    disagreeing with the edge-set client IP.
  Corroborating (40): automation / empty UA; 0x0 screen; implausible screen
    (>8192 px or aspect > 3.8); site suspect-screen list; malformed UA
    platform section (" )").
  Medium (15–25): platform / mobile / brand mismatch; screen vs UA mismatch;
    implausible iOS version
    on a NON-in-app UA; missing accept-language / accept-encoding /
    sec-fetch-mode; iPhone UA with no OS token.
  Low (10): non-WebView Chromium without client hints; outdated Chromium /
    Android; geo nudges. Note: where the Google tag sends hits through its service worker,
    real Chrome arrives without sec-ch-ua, so "chromium-no-client-hints" can
    sit on a large share of genuine purchases — it is noise there, keep it low.
  A clean residential browser scores 0. A residential IP must fail several
  independent integrity checks at once to cross 70.

IN-APP / WEBVIEW BROWSERS ARE NOT BOTS
  Two v2 signals were removed for in-app UAs (INAPP_TOKENS: "; wv)", NAVER,
  FBAN/FBAV/FB_IAB, Instagram, LINE, KakaoTalk, WeChat, TikTok, Snapchat,
  Pinterest, GSA, LinkedIn, WhatsApp, Telegram, ...):
    - Android WebViews report a Chrome/ UA but send no sec-ch-ua. They were
      ~half of purchase revenue on a social-heavy retailer.
    - iOS WebViews commonly FREEZE the OS token at the version the app was
      built against. Observed: "CPU iPhone OS 15_7_2 ... Version/16.5 ...
      NAVER(inapp...)" — a real Safari-16 device carrying a stale iOS-15 token.
      ~26k real users. TikTok's own docs hardcode "iPhone OS 15_7_2" as well.
  Since iOS 26 Apple also freezes the OS token at 18_6/18_7 for privacy; that
  is above IOS_MIN_MAJOR and never flags.

IP RESOLUTION & THE ip_override SPOOF
  The client IP is taken ONLY from the configured trusted edge header (default
  cf-connecting-ip; the LAST comma entry if it is a list), else the socket
  peer. ip_override and X-Forwarded-For are never used to resolve the IP:
  both are client-settable, and Cloudflare APPENDS to an existing XFF, so a
  first-hop read is attacker-controlled.
  Separately, the GA4 client sets ip_override from the request and the GA4 tag
  forwards it to Google as the geolocation IP. If ip_override (public IPv4)
  disagrees with the edge header (public IPv4) and is not the socket peer,
  `ip-override-mismatch` (60) fires. This caught a scraper that made ~5,400
  EU-originated requests/week appear as "Australian" users in GA4 while the CDN
  saw EU IPs and let them through its non-EU block.
  Scoring flags the hit; it does not fix the geo. To stop the spoof reaching
  Google as well: (a) on Cloudflare add a Transform Rule that removes the
  client-supplied X-Forwarded-For before your edge appends its own, and/or
  (b) in the GA4 tag override ip_override with the trusted edge header.
  On a multi-CDN setup where the trusted header is NOT the true client IP,
  set trustedIpHeader to whichever header actually is, or leave it empty.

SEC-FETCH
  Chromium 76+, Firefox 90+ and Safari 16.4+ send sec-fetch-* on fetch/beacon
  requests. Missing sec-fetch-mode on a browser-claiming UA is scored 15. Very
  old real browsers will pick this up; at 15 it cannot block on its own.

SPAM REFERRERS
  Traffic-seller services (trafficheap.cc, sparktraffic, babylontraffic,
  simpletraffic ...) run real headless page loads, often from residential
  proxies the datacenter ranges can't catch — but they advertise themselves in
  page_referrer. The bundled SPAM_REFERRER_DOMAINS list covers known sellers
  plus documented GA4 referral spam; hostname is suffix-matched so subdomains
  count. These domains churn — extend per container via "Extra spam referrer
  domains" and fold recurring ones back into the bundled list.

DIAGNOSTICS MODE (calibration aid)
  Output "Diagnostics" returns a <=100-char fingerprint of what the variable
  saw, so the next weight change is validated in BigQuery instead of guessed.
  Send it as its own GA4 event parameter (e.g. bot_diag) next to a
  reasons-mode variable (bot_reasons); drop both once calibrated.
    sr  screen_resolution as received
    p   client_hints.platform, first 3 letters (and/win/mac/lin/ios/chr);
        "_" = hints present but platform blank; "-" = no client_hints
    m   client_hints.mobile (1/0), "-" if absent
    hv  Chromium major from client_hints brands
    uv  Chrome major from the UA string (hv != uv means a rewritten UA)
    md  client_hints.model present (1/0)
    su  sec-ch-ua request header present (1/0)
    sf  sec-fetch-mode: c cors, n no-cors, s same-origin, v navigate, - none
    al  accept-language header, first tag (compare with ul)
    ul  GA4 language (navigator.language)
    io  ip_override vs trusted edge IP: eq / ne / sk (socket peer) / na
    if  client IP family: 4 / 6
    cc  edge country
    ia  in-app / WebView UA (1/0)
  It carries no IP address and no full UA. Example — fingerprints that never buy:

       SELECT REGEXP_EXTRACT(d, r'p=([^;]*)') AS p, REGEXP_EXTRACT(d, r'm=([^;]*)') AS m,
              REGEXP_EXTRACT(d, r'hv=([^;]*)') = REGEXP_EXTRACT(d, r'uv=([^;]*)') AS ver_match,
              COUNT(DISTINCT user_pseudo_id) AS users,
              COUNTIF(event_name = 'purchase') AS purchases
       FROM (SELECT *, (SELECT value.string_value FROM UNNEST(event_params)
                        WHERE key = 'bot_diag') AS d
             FROM `project.analytics_XXX.events_*`
             WHERE _TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN ... AND ...)
       GROUP BY 1, 2, 3 ORDER BY users DESC;

WHAT THIS CANNOT SEE
  - Hits sent straight to Google's /g/collect with your public measurement
    ID never pass through sGTM and are never scored. Only a server-side-only
    measurement ID (used inside sGTM, absent from page source) closes that.
  - Per-visitor behaviour (one fresh client_id per page, identical event
    sequences) — the variable is stateless per hit. Detect that in BigQuery.
  - TLS fingerprints (JA3/JA4) — CDN-edge only; on Cloudflare they need
    Enterprise Bot Management.
  - IPv6 clients are not range-matched (fall through to header/UA signals).

BUNDLED RANGES
  DATACENTER_RANGES + AI_CRAWLER_RANGES are generated from the providers'
  official published lists (AWS, GCP, plus seeded Azure/DO/Hetzner/OVH/Linode/
  Vultr super-blocks; OpenAI GPTBot/ChatGPT-User/SearchBot, Googlebot and
  Google special crawlers). Refresh them periodically with build-ranges.mjs.
  CloudFront / Global Accelerator ranges are intentionally NOT included so a
  visitor transiting them is not mistaken for a bot. Anthropic ClaudeBot and
  PerplexityBot publish no stable machine-readable range list, so they are
  matched by UA only (not IP-verifiable).

